  to incorporate real world issues into  its narrative  bucky is clearly suffering from ptsd and  being unable to sleep is one of the most  significant health concerns  veterans have to face after returning  from deployment  after meeting the flag smashers in  episode 2 and discovering their super  soldiers  bucky takes sam to meet isaiah bradley  to discover how the super soldier serum  might have returned  isaiah is a super soldier who in the  comics was the second person to become  captain america  isaiah bradley was one of a handful of  black soldiers who were experimented on  in the 1950s  and given different variations of  potential super soldier serum  replacements  the scientists lied to them telling them  they were being given tetanus vaccines  before sending them off on missions in  the korean war  during which isaiah even managed to  defeat and literally disarm the winter  soldier  this narrative is the exact premise  behind the real world tuskegee syphilis  study  a medical research project that examined  the effects of syphilis  over the course of 40 years by  purposefully injecting hundreds of black  men  in order to observe the cause of the  disease throughout their lives  they were lied to just like isaiah was  and as a result  more than a hundred of the men died  because of the study and many of their  partners were unwittingly infected with  the disease  as well during his talk with sam isaiah  also directly references  men in the red tails the famous 332  these are the real world tuskegee airmen  the famed all black fighter squadron who  fought in world war  ii isaiah mentions how they didn't come  home to fame or thanks  but to racist persecution after meeting  isaiah bucky and sam begin walking  through the neighborhood  as sam struggles with what he's just  learned their discussion is stopped in  his tracks when two policemen pull up  ask bucky if sam is bothering him  and then demand to see sam's id the  police don't back down until bucky  finally points out samus a falcon  and one of the police officers  recognizes them as avengers  the police stand very calmly politely  and apologetically arrest bucky for  missing his court-mandated therapy  session  a stark contrast to the hostility they  had just shown sam  racial profiling is one of america's  oldest problems and this scene is an  excellent example of how inescapable it  can be a person's appearance  status and clothing doesn't matter it  can happen to anyone  and there are many real world examples  that show this the esteemed georgia  congressman john lewis was arrested more  than 40 times  throughout his life even after he became  a member of congress  in 2009 harvard professor dr henry lewis  gates jr was arrested after accidentally  locking himself out of his house  more recently a black army officer in  virginia was threatened by police  officers  and arrested in virginia while in  uniform  by far the most shocking image in the  entirety of the falcon and the winter  soldier  is the shot at the end of episode 4 the  whole world is watching  john walker is standing and staring at  hundreds of people while holding captain  america's shield  now covered in the blood of the  defenseless an armed man he just  murdered in a heated rage in broad  daylight  until this moment marvel has taken great  pain to portray captain america and his  shield  as symbols of pure heroism that  represent the highest ideals that  america often perports to stand for  by literally bloodying that very symbol  the image reminds the viewers of the  many ways that america has fallen short  of those values throughout its existence  more specifically the entire sequence is  reminiscent of one of the most  constant news topics of the last year  police violence in america  and the use of excessive force and  violence walker killing a citizen of a  foreign nation in a place where he  likely had little or no jurisdiction  also calls to mind the many instances of  american interference and foreign policy  in episode 5 john walker loses the  shield is found guilty by military court  and is stripped of the title of captain  america during the hearing he lashes out  at the panel  and exclaims that he lived his life by  and dedicated it to their mandates  that he had been what they told him to  be and done what they had trained him to  do  you built me in walker's eyes the  government is punishing him for doing  exactly what they wanted  not for murdering a man but for doing it  where others could see  for saying the quiet part out loud this  continues the idea that  unlike steve rogers walker's captain  america symbolizes the often dark  reality  of what america is rather than the dream  of what it could be  john walker's crumbling mental state and  his feelings of abandonment in that  scene  also speak to common failures of care  that veterans experience  when they return from deployment  veterans with post-traumatic stress  disorder are often left untreated  and many are completely unaware of how  to access the mental health resources  they have at their disposal in episode 5  a brief look at a painting is used as a  transition shot before showing sharon  carter speaking on the phone  the surface implication is that this is  one of the many paintings that carter  has to sell on the black market  but as reddit user guy erebus explains  there is a wealth of symbolism behind  the use of this painting  and its relation to the themes of the  series the raft of the medusa by  theodore jericho  depicts the aftermath of the wreck of  the medusa a french navy vessel that ran  aground in 1816  due to incompetence of his captain he  would only receive the position because  of his connections to the french  government  the captain used the ship's only  lifeboats to save himself and the other  officers  leaving the working-class sailors to  starve on a hastily made raft for 13  days  before they were finally rescued when  the painting was unveiled in 1819  it proved highly controversial for many  of the same themes  embracing the falcon and the winter  soldier the painting's apex features a  black man working hand-in-hand with a  white man while waving a flag  making its feature in this show quite  appropriate additionally john walker is  literally an  incompetent captain chosen by a corrupt  government who are withholding resources  from and persecuting the working-class  refugees created by the blip  when sam wilson debuted in captain  america the winter soldier  the first and most telling trait they  established for his character was that  he was a councillor for veterans  this propels the entirety of his first  conversation with steve  and forms a significant part of the  foundation of their friendship  steve even seems to want to sam in  avengers end game by working as a  counselor himself  the series uses this background to shape  sam's character  and his responses to the events  surrounding him when he first meets  carly he empathizes with her  and uses his training as a counselor in  an attempt to talk her down  and convince her to pursue her goals  without violence similarly when he  practices  using the shield with bucky in episode 5  sam helps him work through his issues  you got to stop looking to other people  to tell you who you are  [Music]  these scenes are some of the best  moments in the series showing audiences  exactly why samuel makes such a good  captain america  as he says in episode 6 he believes in  the good in people and his  is that he genuinely believes we can all  do better  after his debut as the new captain  america at the end of the series  sam wilson visits isaiah bradley once  again and explains why he took up the  shield  some explains that after the pain and  suffering people like isaiah had  gone through before sam that after so  many african americans had bled for the  united states  he was not about to let anyone tell him  that he couldn't fight for his country  we built this country bled for it  the 13 british colonies that would  eventually become the united states were  created  in part with the use of slave labor when  those 13 colonies became states their  struggling economy was boosted  and aided by the now extensive and  entrenched use of slave labor  especially in the south this even  continued after the american civil war  via the practice of sharecropping the  falcon in the winter soldier has  multiple themes running throughout its  six episodes  but at the heart of it all is one  question who makes someone a good  captain america  by focusing on this theme the show pays  homage to cap's mcu debut in the first  avenger  everything leading to steve's  transformation into a super soldier  revolves around what makes someone a  good choice for the program  as dr erskine says the serum takes what  is already inside a person  and amplifies it by giving them the  power to finally act as they've always  wished to  good becomes great bet  becomes worse this is why dr roskine  rejects one soldier early in the film  though he was a great soldier  he was a bully who would likely abuse  any power he was given  dr erskine urges steve to remain quote  not a perfect soldier  but a good man these ideas force the  foundation of the entire series  and explain why when all is said and  done sam wilson is captain america  the government chose john walker because  he was the perfect soldier  and it played out exactly as dr erskine  had feared  even bucky couldn't do it because he's  still trying to figure out who he is  after spending decades as a winter  soldier  sam wilson however is a good humble  person who just wants to help everybody  he meets become better people  it's hard to argue with the idea that  we'd all like to see that character  trait amplified  in those around us check out one of our  newest videos right here plus  even more looper videos about the falcon  and the winter soldier are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss  a single one  [Music]

